 "area": "retina",
 "description": "Async 2-D MOTION from the onset-event stream (the 2-D expansion of Rung 2, closing the 2-D loop after 166000). Rungs 1-4 read azimuth (column_index) ONLY, so a PURELY VERTICAL mover -- onset events at ONE azimuth column, its rows sweeping over successive epochs -- has zero azimuth spatial spread and is INVISIBLE to the 1-D retinal_event_motion (speed 0, coherence 0). Here the events carry (column_index=az, row_index=el, epoch) and the velocity is recovered in BOTH axes: the `retinal_event_motion_2d` Rust task kind computes v_az = least-squares slope of col on epoch and v_el = slope of row on epoch, output = [v_az, v_el, dir_az, dir_el, speed=|(v_az,v_el)|, residual, coherence, n] where coherence is the combined multiple-correlation that reduces EXACTLY to the 1-D |Pearson r| when there is no elevation spread and is 1.0 for a noiseless sweep in ANY direction including pure elevation. This rule mints a RetinalMotionPercept (carrying v_az, v_el, direction, speed, residual, coherence) when coherence (output[6]) and event count (output[7]) exceed thresholds held as DATA on a RetinaMotionParams2d node. A static scene mints no events; a scrambled-epoch set decorrelates (coherence -> 0 -> no percept). OFF by default: the rule cannot fire unless a RetinaMotionParams2d node exists (created by the adapter only when context['visual_motion'] AND context['retina_2d'] are set), and the adapter creates the 2-D motion task only then. The 1-D retinal_event_motion kind + retinal_motion seed are byte-untouched.",
 "microtheory": {
  "name": "retinal_motion_2d",
  "description": "2-D space-time slope of (az,el) onset events -> async 2-D motion percept (v_az, v_el)"
 },
